# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nova backend — environment variables
# Copy this file to ".env" in the same folder and fill in real keys.
# Nothing here is committed to git (.env is in .gitignore).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PORT=8787

# Where the frontend dev server runs — used for CORS. Leave as-is for local dev.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ── Nova's brain (reasoning/tool-calling), voice (GPT-Live-1), AND live web
# search (weather, sports scores/schedules, news — anything current) — all
# from ONE OpenAI key now ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Get a key at https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
#
# History: Claude Haiku 4.5 ($1/$5 per 1M in/out tokens) → briefly tried Groq
# (free tier hard-caps at 8,000 tokens/minute; Nova's own tool schemas alone
# need ~21k, so every request failed outright) → Gemini (works, but its
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint has no built-in search tool at all) → reverted
# to Anthropic once credits were added → swapped again to gpt-4.1-mini for
# real, lasting cost savings (~2.5-3x cheaper than Haiku) without losing web
# search. CONFIRMED BY RESEARCH: gpt-4o-mini (the obvious cheapest pick) is a
# dead end — its search-capable variant was deprecated and shut down
# 2026-07-23, and plain gpt-4o-mini has no path to the Responses API's
# built-in web_search tool at all, which would silently break weather/
# sports/news the exact same way the Gemini attempt did. gpt-4.1-mini is the
# one that's both current and meaningfully cheaper.
#
# Web search is OpenAI's own built-in Responses API tool (not a separate
# API/key) — resolved server-side, billed on top of normal token costs.
# GPT-Live-1 is the ONLY thing that listens and speaks — there's no fallback
# voice pipeline, so Nova can't hear or talk without this key being set
# either way.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_LIVE_MODEL=gpt-live-1
OPENAI_REASONING_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini

# ── Composio — connects Nova to Gmail, Google Calendar, and anything else in
# their tool catalog ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONFIRMED BY TESTING (curl against backend.composio.dev/api/v3 directly,
# Sept 2026): Composio has TWO differently-prefixed keys that are easy to
# grab the wrong one of — get this wrong and every request fails with a
# generic "Invalid API key" that doesn't say WHY:
#   - ck_... = a "Connect consumer key" (Connect dashboard → Settings →
#     Sessions & API Key) — for MCP consumer sessions, NOT this.
#   - ak_... = the "Project API key" (Developer dashboard → Project Settings
#     → API Keys) — THIS is what belongs here.
# No toolkit allowlist needed here — whatever you connect through the
# Connectors page (search covers Composio's whole ~1,500-toolkit catalog) is
# automatically usable, no config change required.
COMPOSIO_API_KEY=

# ── Timezone Nova uses to resolve "this Thursday at 4" etc. ────────────────
# Leave blank to use the server machine's own timezone automatically, or set
# an IANA name, e.g. TIMEZONE=America/Chicago
TIMEZONE=

# ── Twilio — gives every Nova account a phone number they can text AND call.
# Chosen over Inkbox: same idea (an "identity layer" for an agent), but
# Twilio is the established, cheaper, purpose-built platform. ─────────────
# Get these at https://console.twilio.com:
#   - TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID / TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN — Account dashboard home page.
#   - TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER — buy a number with BOTH SMS and Voice capability
#     (Phone Numbers → Buy a number), in E.164 form, e.g. +15551234567. This
#     is the ONE shared number every user texts/calls — routing to the right
#     account happens by the caller/sender's own number (the `From` field),
#     not by which number they reached, so it works the same for everyone.
# Then, on that number's configuration page, set:
#   - Messaging → "A message comes in" → {PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/api/sms/webhook (HTTP POST)
#   - Voice → "A call comes in" → {PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/api/voice-call/incoming (HTTP POST)
# Nova can also call OUT on her own (make_phone_call tool, services/outboundCall.ts)
# to accomplish something over the phone for the user — no console setup needed
# for this part, it's all driven by the Calls.json REST API at request time,
# pointed at /api/voice-call/outbound-twiml (answered) and
# /api/voice-call/outbound-status (never answered/busy/failed).
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=

# The public URL Twilio's webhook can reach this server at. Twilio can't POST
# to localhost directly — for local dev, run `ngrok http 8787` and put its
# https://...ngrok-free.app URL here (update it and the Twilio console's
# webhook URL every time ngrok gives you a new one, unless you're on a paid
# ngrok plan with a fixed domain). In production this is your real domain.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
